      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate of Conformity for turbine gas meters:\n- Number: R137/2012-NL1-14.03, revision 1\n- Project number: SO15202424\n- Page total: 5\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 31 July 2015\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman, Head Certification Board\n- Address: Hugo de Grootplein 1, 3314 EG Dordrecht, the Netherlands\n- Phone: +31 78 6332332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- Applicant: Flow Meter Group B.V., Meniststraat 5c, 7091 ZZ Dinxperlo, The Netherlands\n- Manufacturer: same\n\nCertified type: turbine gas meter\nTypes: FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx and FMT-Dc\n\nRecommendation: R 137-1 (2012) \"Gas meters\" → canonical id \"R137\", edition 2012? Hmm. R137-1 (2012). The canonical id is R137; edition... The year 2012 could be treated as edition? For R137, the edition year is 2012. Part 1. Let me use edition: 2012, amendment: null, maybe note part 1. The schema says edition (int). I'll put 2012. Accuracy classes: 1 (class 1). Scheme: A (certificates of conformity under OIML CS are scheme A). Actually OIML-CS has Scheme A (certificate) and Scheme B (type approval). OIML certificates of conformity issued by issuing authorities like NMi are Scheme A. Set scheme: \"A\".\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-14200104-01, 15 September 2014, 14 pages, role: type_evaluation_report (these are OIML Type Evaluation Reports)\n- NMi-14200702-01, 18 December 2014, 13 pages\n- NMi-14200712-02, 23 December 2014, 35 pages\n- VSL-3255499-01, 28 January 2015, 6 pages\n\nDocumentation folder no. T10466-6 → test_reports entry with role documentation_file, id \"T10466-6\". Date not given. Omit date? The rule says don't fabricate. I'll include id and role.\n\nCharacteristics from page 2 (Table 1 general characteristics, applying to all types):\n- Destined for the measurement of: Gas volume\n- Mechanical class: M2\n- Electromagnetic class: Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\n- Ambient temperature range: -40 °C / +70 °C → {min: -40, max: 70}, unit °C\n- Gas temperature range: -40 °C / +70 °C → same\n- Orientation: Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations)\n- Flow direction: Uni-directional (indicated with arrow)\n\nThese are type-level characteristics (apply to all models).\n\nPage 3 Table 1: The big matrix table. Columns:\n- Type\n- DN body (mm)\n- DN cartr. (mm)\n- G-value (-)\n- Qmax (m³/h)\n- Qt (m³/h)\n- Qmin MR 1:20 (m³/h) — condition 0..101 bar(g)\n- Qmin MR 1:30 (m³/h) — condition 4..101 bar(g)\n- Qmin MR 1:50 (m³/h) — condition 8..101 bar(g)\n- Number of drums Before and after comma (-)\n- Control element (m³/h)\n- Blade angle (degree)\n- Blade qty (-)\n- Bearing dimensions Inside diameter x Outside diameter x Width in mm: front standard bearings 0..101 bar(g), rear standard bearings 0..101 bar(g), front optional bearings 4..101 bar(g), rear optional bearings 4..101 bar(g)\n\nWait, let me re-read the header structure. The header has:\n- Row: Type | DN body | DN cartr. | G-value | Qmax | Qt | Qmin (colspan 3) | Number of drums Before and after comma | Control element | Blade angle | Blade qty | Bearing dimensions (colspan 4 rowspan 2) \"Inside diameter x Outside diameter x Width in mm\"\n- Row 2: MR 1:20 | MR 1:30 | MR 1:50\n- Row 3: 0..101 bar(g) | 4..101 bar(g) | 8..101 bar(g) | standard bearings 0..101 bar(g) (colspan 2) | Optional bearings 4..101 bar(g) (colspan 2)\n- Units row: - | mm | mm | - | m³/h | m³/h | m³/h | m³/h | m³/h | - | m³/h | degree | - | front | rear | front | rear\n\nHmm wait — the alignment. There are 17 columns total (colspan 17 in first th). Let me count the columns:\n1. Type\n2. DN body\n3. DN cartr.\n4. G-value\n5. Qmax\n6. Qt\n7. Qmin MR 1:20 (0..101 bar(g))\n8. Qmin MR 1:30 (4..101 bar(g))\n9. Qmin MR 1:50 (8..101 bar(g))\n10. Number of drums before and after comma\n11. Control element\n12. Blade angle\n13. Blade qty\n14. Bearing dimensions front (standard bearings 0..101 bar(g))\n15. Bearing dimensions rear (standard bearings 0..101 bar(g))\n16. Bearing dimensions front (optional bearings 4..101 bar(g))\n17. Bearing dimensions rear (optional bearings 4..101 bar(g))\n\nUnits row: \"-\" | mm | mm | \"-\" | m³/h | m³/h | m³/h | m³/h | m³/h | \"-\" | m³/h | degree | \"-\" | front | rear | front | rear\n\nSo bearing dimension columns are labeled front/rear, and each has dimensions in mm as \"3x6x3\" format strings (inside diameter x outside diameter x width).\n\nHmm, but wait: the standard bearings vs optional bearings mapping. The header row 3 says: \"standard bearings 0..101 bar(g)\" colspan 2, \"Optional bearings 4..101 bar(g)\" colspan 2. So columns 14-15 are standard bearings front/rear, columns 16-17 are optional bearings front/rear. The units row says front/rear/front/rear which is odd since the bearing dims sub-header already says that. Actually maybe the units row \"front rear front rear\" corresponds to front/rear within standard and front/rear within optional. OK.\n\nData rows: Each type group spans 3 rows with rowspan for Type, DN body, DN cartr., Number of drums, Control element, Bearing dims.\n\nLet me enumerate the row groups:\n\nGroup 1: Type \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", DN body 50, DN cartr. 80, drums 7 / 1, control element 0,02, bearings: 3x6x3 (std front), 2x5x2 (std rear), 3x8x4 (opt front), 3x6x3 (opt rear)\n- Row 1: G=100, Qmax=160, Qt=32, Qmin(1:20)=8, Qmin(1:30)=5, Qmin(1:50)=3, blade angle 55, blade qty 12\n- Row 2: G=160, Qmax=250, Qt=50, Qmin=12.5, 8, 5, blade 45, qty 14\n- Row 3: G=250, Qmax=400, Qt=80, Qmin=20, 12.5, 8, blade 35, qty 16\n\nGroup 2: Type \"FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", DN body 80, DN cartr. 80, same as above values, bearings 3x6x3, 2x5x2, 3x8x4, 3x6x3\n\nGroup 3: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 80/100:\n- G160: 250, 50, 12.5, 8, 5; blade 55, 12\n- G250: 400, 80, 20, 12.5, 8; blade 45, 14\n- G400: 650, 130, 32.5, 20, 12.5; blade 35, 16\nbearings: 3x8x4, 3x6x3, 5x10x3, 3x6x3; drums 7/1, control 0,02\n\nGroup 4: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 100/80: same as group 1 values (G100/160/250), bearings 3x6x3, 2x5x2, 3x8x4, 3x6x3\n\nGroup 5: FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 100/100: same as group 3 values (G160/250/400), bearings 3x8x4, 3x6x3, 5x10x3, 3x6x3\n\nGroup 6: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 100/150:\n- G400: 650, 130, 32.5, 20, 12.5; drums 7/1, control 0,02; blade 55, 12\n- G650: 1000, 200, 50, 32, 20; blade 45, 14\n- G1000: 1600, 320, 80, 50, 32; drums 8 / 0, control 0,2; blade 35, 16\nbearings: 5x11x5, 5x11x5, 5x16x5, 5x11x5\n\nGroup 7: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 150/100: same as group 3 values (G160/250/400), bearings 3x8x4, 3x6x3, 5x10x3, 3x6x3\n\nGroup 8: FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 150/150: same as group 6 (G400/650/1000), bearings 5x11x5, 5x11x5, 5x16x5, 5x11x5\n\nGroup 9: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 150/200:\n- G650: 1000, 200, 50, 32, 20; drums 7/1, control 0,02; blade 55, 12\n- G1000: 1600, 320, 80, 50, 32; drums 8/0, control 0,2 (rowspan 2); blade 45, 14\n- G1600: 2500, 500, 125, 80, 50; blade 35, 16\nbearings: 5x16x5, 5x16x5, 8x22x7, 5x16x5\n\nGroup 10: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 200/150: same as group 6 (G400/650/1000), bearings 5x11x5 etc.\n\nGroup 11: FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 200/200: same as group 9 (G650/1000/1600), bearings 5x16x5, 5x16x5, 8x22x7, 5x16x5\n\nGroup 12: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 250/200: same as group 9 (G650/1000/1600), bearings 5x16x5, 5x16x5, 8x22x7, 5x16x5\n\nSo this is a big matrix table. I should preserve it in `matrix_tables[]` and also populate model_family and model_level.\n\nHow to model? The models here are combinations of Type + DN body + G-value. Each G-value corresponds to a specific Qmax/Qt/Qmin set. The models could be identified by G-value (G100, G160, G250, G400, G650, G1000, G1600). Actually G-values: 100, 160, 250, 400, 650, 1000, 1600.\n\nModel family: FMT turbine gas meter family with types FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc. Models could be keyed by size/body type combos. This is complex. The schema example for R137 used size G1.6, G2.5 with models keyed by size. Here the natural model axis is Type × DN × G-value. I could define model_id like \"FMT-Lx DN50 G100\" etc. That's 12 groups × 3 rows = 36 model variants. That's a lot but doable.\n\nAlternatively, keep the full table in matrix_tables and only populate model_level for the varying attributes keyed by model_id. Given the rules (\"Detect this and put varying attributes in model_level\"), and the R137 example populated both matrix_tables and model_level.\n\nLet me build model_ids. I'll use format \"FMT-Lx DN50 G100\" — hmm, but the DN body and cartridge both matter. E.g. \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\". Model id strings can be descriptive.\n\nActually, let me think about what's cleanest:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"FMT turbine gas meter\" (the types are FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx, FMT-Dc; \"FMT\" is the family name). I'll use \"FMT\".\n- models: one entry per combination. 36 entries with attributes type, dn_body, dn_cartridge, g_value, qmax, qt, qmin per MR, number_of_drums, control_element, blade_angle, blade_qty, bearing dimensions.\n\nHmm, that's heavy. Alternatively, models keyed more simply and put per-variant attributes into model_level arrays.\n\nI think a reasonable approach:\n\nmodels[]: each entry {model_id, label, attributes{...}} where attributes include type_designation, dn_body_mm, dn_cartridge_mm, g_value, qmax_m3h, qt_m3h, qmin_mr1_20_m3h, qmin_mr1_30_m3h, qmin_mr1_50_m3h, number_of_drums, control_element_m3h, blade_angle_degree, blade_qty, bearing_front_standard, bearing_rear_standard, bearing_front_optional, bearing_rear_optional.\n\nThat's essentially duplicating matrix_tables. The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables, and model_level is attribute × model matrix. To avoid over-bloating, I could:\n\n1. Put the full table in matrix_tables with 36 rows.\n2. Create model_family with 36 models with minimal attributes (model_id, label, attributes with type, dn_body, dn_cartridge, g_value).\n3. model_level entries for qmax, qt, qmin (per meter ratio condition), blade_angle, blade_qty, control_element, number_of_drums, bearing dimensions.\n\nHmm, qmin varies by both model AND meter ratio (MR 1:20, 1:30, 1:50). Actually MR (meter ratio) might be a configuration axis. Qmin depends on the ratio: for MR 1:20, Qmin = Qmax/20; 1:30 → Qmax/30; 1:50 → Qmax/50. The bar(g) conditions are the pressure ranges under which each meter ratio is valid: MR 1:20 valid 0..101 bar(g), MR 1:30 valid 4..101 bar(g), MR 1:50 valid 8..101 bar(g).\n\nHmm wait, actually re-reading: the MR header row and the pressure header row. \"MR 1:20\" with \"0..101 bar(g)\", \"MR 1:30\" with \"4..101 bar(g)\", \"MR 1:50\" with \"8..101 bar(g)\". These are the operating pressure ranges for which each meter ratio is approved? That seems to be it.\n\nSo Qmin is a config-level attribute with axis \"meter_ratio\" and condition describing MR and pressure range. I could model it as three separate attributes: qmin_meter_ratio_1_20, qmin_meter_ratio_1_30, qmin_meter_ratio_1_50, each with model_level values. Or config_level with axis \"meter_ratio\". The schema's config_level: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]}. Config-level doesn't have a model dimension though. Since qmin varies by both model and MR, the cleanest is three model_level attributes named by MR. Alternatively put Qmin into matrix tables only. I'll do three model_level attributes: `qmin_meter_ratio_1_20`, `qmin_meter_ratio_1_30`, `qmin_meter_ratio_1_50` with a note. And maybe also a config_level entry documenting pressure ranges per MR. Actually let me add config_level:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"qmin_meter_ratio_validity_pressure_range\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter_ratio\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"MR 1:20\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 101}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"MR 1:30\", \"value\": {\"min\": 4, \"max\": 101}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"MR 1:50\", \"value\": {\"min\": 8, \"max\": 101}, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar(g)\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nHmm, \"bar(g)\" as unit symbol — gauge pressure. Fine.\n\nNow the bearing dimensions: columns \"front\"/\"rear\" under standard (0..101 bar(g)) and optional (4..101 bar(g)). These vary by DN group (they're constant across the 3 G rows within a group). Values like \"3x6x3\" — dimensions inside diameter x outside diameter x width in mm. These are strings. I could add them as model_level attributes with string values, or components (bearings as essential parts). The table header says \"1.1 Essential parts\" includes Bearing dimensions, Blade angle, Blade qty. Essential parts could go to components[]. Actually the header categorizes:\n- Main characteristics: Type, DN body, DN cartr., G-value\n- 1.2 Essential characteristics: Qmax, Qt, Qmin\n- 1.4 Conditional parts: Number of drums, Control element\n- 1.1 Essential parts: Blade angle, Blade qty, Bearing dimensions\n\nHmm interesting. So I could create components for essential parts:\n- role: \"essential_part\", blade... but blades vary per model. Bearings vary per DN group.\n\nMaybe simpler: keep everything in matrix_tables + model_level. Components with per-model characteristics get messy. But the schema says components[] include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies. The cert names \"Control element\" (value 0,02 or 0,2 m³/h), bearings with dimensions, blade angle/qty. These are parts of the meter. I could add a component \"rotor blade\" with characteristics varying... no, characteristics in components aren't per-model in the schema (characteristics{} is a free object). I could include blade info as component characteristics but since they vary per model, better keep in model_level/matrix.\n\nDecision: keep blade and bearing data in matrix_tables and model_level. Skip components[] since per-model variation dominates; or add a components entry for bearings with role and characteristics describing formats. Hmm. Rule 6 says record each as components[] entry for complex instruments with named sub-assemblies. The cert names essential parts (blades, bearings, control element/drum). I think it's justified to add components entries but the per-model values live in model_level. Let me add:\n\ncomponents:\n- {role: \"essential_part\", type_designations: [\"Rotor blade\"], characteristics: {blade_angle: \"...\", blade_qty: \"...\"}} — but values vary. I'd rather not put single values that misrepresent. Maybe omit components. The instruction \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\" — it does name them (bearing, blades, drums, control element). But given the variation, I'll include components with descriptive characteristics pointing to the matrix, or with alternatives. Hmm.\n\nI'll include components entries without fabricating single values:\n- {role: \"essential_part\", type_designations: [\"Blade (rotor)\"], characteristics: {note: \"Blade angle and quantity vary per model; see matrix_tables flow_characteristics_by_size\"}}\nHmm, that's noise. Let me just skip components[] — cleaner and avoids fabricated structure. Actually wait — \"Control element\" with values 0,02 m³/h and 0,2 m³/h and \"Number of drums 7 / 1\" and \"8 / 0\" — these are conditional parts (1.4). The control element value 0.02 m³/h seems to be a capacity/threshold. The drums \"before and after comma\" = 7/1 (seven drums before decimal point, one after). These vary by model (high G rows have 8/0 and 0,2).\n\nI'll keep all in model_level attributes:\n- number_of_drums_before_and_after_decimal_point: string \"7 / 1\" or \"8 / 0\"\n- control_element: 0.02 or 0.2, unit m³/h\n- blade_angle: 55/45/35, unit degree\n- blade_quantity: 12/14/16\n- bearing dimensions: strings like \"3x6x3\" for front/rear standard/optional.\n\nModel IDs: Let me construct them as \"FMT-Lx DN50/80 G100\"? Wait, for groups where type is \"FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", the model applies to all four types. Model id could be \"DN50/80 G100 (FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc)\". Hmm.\n\nAlternative: model_id = \"DN{body}/{cartr}-G{value}\" with attributes type applicability. E.g. \"DN50/80-G100\". That's compact and unique. Let me check uniqueness: group1 DN50/80 G100,160,250; group2 DN80/80 G100,160,250 (different types allowed); group3 DN80/100 G160,250,400; group4 DN100/80 G100,160,250; group5 DN100/100 G160,250,400; group6 DN100/150 G400,650,1000; group7 DN150/100 G160,250,400; group8 DN150/150 G400,650,1000; group9 DN150/200 G650,1000,1600; group10 DN200/150 G400,650,1000; group11 DN200/200 G650,1000,1600; group12 DN250/200 G650,1000,1600.\n\nAll unique with DN prefix: DN50/80-G100, DN80/80-G100, DN80/100-G160, DN100/80-G100, DN100/100-G160, DN100/150-G400, DN150/100-G160, DN150/150-G400, DN150/200-G650, DN200/150-G400, DN200/200-G650, DN250/200-G650 etc. Good.\n\nSo 36 models total.\n\nmodel_level attributes and values per model (36 values each — that's large but correct). Let me decide which attributes to emit at model_level: qmax, qt, qmin (three MR variants), blade_angle, blade_quantity, control_element, number_of_drums, bearing dims (4 attributes), dn_body, dn_cartridge, g_value, type_applicability.\n\nHmm, that's 13 attributes × 36 values = 468 value objects. That's very verbose. Maybe better to put full data in matrix_tables rows (36 rows) and keep model_level to the key flow characteristics (qmax, qt, qmin ×3) as the schema example did. The R137 example had model_level only for qmax, qt, qmin, and the rest in matrix_tables. I'll follow that pattern: model_level for qmax, qt, qmin (MR-specific). And model_family.models entries include attributes {type, dn_body_mm, dn_cartridge_mm, g_value} — enough to identify. The rest (drums, control element, blades, bearings) live in matrix_tables rows.\n\nActually, let me include in models[].attributes: type, dn_body_mm, dn_cartridge_mm, g_value. And label: e.g. \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 50/80, G 100\".\n\nNow matrix_tables:\n\nTable 1 (page 2): general characteristics — that's not really